  "body": "Adds an inactive PostgreSQL social authority with independently owned connection consent, directed follows, blocking/muting/dismissal, revisions and retry identity. Disconnect removes only the caller's intent; block clears both, and unblock restores neither. Existing Party, fan-club, membership and content authorities remain intact.\n\nDepends on #355 at reviewed audit 6d5c25c20. Draft and unmerged; activation is prohibited until legacy privacy enforcement is integrated.\n\nVerification: the updated executable ConsentTraces model explored 32 states and generated 30 passing SQL transitions. It exposed a real old-SQL counterexample (state 23 erased peer consent), which is repaired here. The private PostgreSQL fixture passed retries/payload conflicts, rate limits, block/accept lock race, closure, read eligibility and migration reapplication/pause. Logs, source DOT and generated assertions are committed. Later #367 adds actual authenticated HTTP coverage and full-model rerun evidence.\n\nApply the two additive social_v2 SQL files only in reviewed non-production fixtures; they are not boot migrations. The database runtime defaults off; process/client gates are added in #367 and also default off. Pause preserves every new consent/block/tombstone/publication write. Do not revert an activated application to legacy routes that ignore block state. Full-schema rehearsal, token/delegated identity, private requests, notifications/media/DM lifecycle integration and production index-lock qualification remain incomplete. No graph service or broker is introduced.\n",
